The document skeleton an author writes — sections, navigation, and the horizontal rule.

<section #sh-section | `<section>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *section*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<section>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<section>`
- Sigil: `<# … #>`
- Markdown: `# …`

<code #code:sh-section-1>
<section | Introduction>
The paper begins here.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-section-1>
<section | Introduction>
The paper begins here.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-section-2>
<section #methods sec-type=methods | Methods>
<section-subtitle | A description of our experimental approach>
The methods used in this study were as follows.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-section-2>
<section #methods sec-type=methods | Methods>
<section-subtitle | A description of our experimental approach>
The methods used in this study were as follows.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-section-3>
<section | Results>
Results paragraph.

<sub-section | Quantitative analysis>
Sub-section content.

<sub-section | Qualitative observations>
Sub-section content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-section-3>
<section | Results>
Results paragraph.

<sub-section | Quantitative analysis>
Sub-section content.

<sub-section | Qualitative observations>
Sub-section content.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `sec-type` | kwarg | `intro` `methods` `results` `discussion` `conclusion` `supplementary` `materials` `references` `other` | Optional classification of the section's role. Maps to JATS sec-type attribute. Values match common JATS conventions for IMRaD-style papers. |
| `numbering-style` | kwarg | `arabic` `roman` `alpha` `none` | Override the inherited numbering style for this section. |
| `+unlisted` | boolean | default `false` | Keep this section out of the generated table of contents, regardless of toc-depth (#218). Display-only: the section still renders; it is only absent from the contents listing. The ToC is config-driven (<config toc=true>); see notes/specs/toc-and-numbering.md. Authored as +unlisted (the boolean shorthand); renders to the HTML attribute unlisted. |
| `+unnumbered` | boolean | default `false` | Skip this section's number, regardless of number-depth (#218). The heading is OUTSIDE the numbered sequence — it gets no number AND does not advance the counter, so the next numbered sibling continues unbroken (the \\section* / Quarto .unnumbered behavior); its subtree is unnumbered too. Numbering is config-driven (<config number-sections=true>); see notes/specs/toc-and-numbering.md. Authored as +unnumbered; the number stamp reads node.booleans.unnumbered in runSync, and the unnumbered attribute also renders on the element. |

</section>

<section #sh-sub-section | `<sub-section>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *sub-section*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<sub-section>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<sub-section>`
- Sigil: `<## … ##>`
- Markdown: `## …`

<code #code:sh-sub-section-1>
<sub-section | Quantitative analysis>
Sub-section content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-section-1>
<sub-section | Quantitative analysis>
Sub-section content.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sub-section-2>
<section | Results>
<sub-section | Statistical methods>
Sub-section content.

<sub-sub-section | Regression analysis>
Sub-sub-section content.

<sub-section | Sensitivity analyses>
Sub-section content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-section-2>
<section | Results>
<sub-section | Statistical methods>
Sub-section content.

<sub-sub-section | Regression analysis>
Sub-sub-section content.

<sub-section | Sensitivity analyses>
Sub-section content.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `sec-type` | kwarg | `intro` `methods` `results` `discussion` `conclusion` `supplementary` `materials` `references` `other` | Same values as <section>. Optional classification of the sub-section role. |
| `numbering-style` | kwarg | `arabic` `roman` `alpha` `none` |  |
| `+unlisted` | boolean | default `false` | Keep this sub-section out of the generated table of contents, regardless of toc-depth (#218). Display-only: it still renders; it is only absent from the contents listing. See notes/specs/toc-and-numbering.md. Authored as +unlisted; renders to the HTML attribute unlisted. |
| `+unnumbered` | boolean | default `false` | Skip this sub-section's number, regardless of number-depth (#218). Outside the numbered sequence — no number, no counter advance, subtree unnumbered; the next numbered sibling continues unbroken. See notes/specs/toc-and-numbering.md. Authored as +unnumbered. |

</section>

<section #sh-sub-sub-section | `<sub-sub-section>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *sub-sub-section*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<sub-sub-section>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<sub-sub-section>`
- Sigil: `<### … ###>`
- Markdown: `### …`

<code #code:sh-sub-sub-section-1>
<sub-sub-section | Regression analysis>
Detailed methods for the regression.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-sub-section-1>
<sub-sub-section | Regression analysis>
Detailed methods for the regression.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sub-sub-section-2>
<section | Methods>
<sub-section | Statistical methods>
<sub-sub-section | Regression>
Linear regression was performed.

<sub-sub-section | Sensitivity testing>
Sensitivity tests were performed.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-sub-section-2>
<section | Methods>
<sub-section | Statistical methods>
<sub-sub-section | Regression>
Linear regression was performed.

<sub-sub-section | Sensitivity testing>
Sensitivity tests were performed.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `sec-type` | kwarg | `intro` `methods` `results` `discussion` `conclusion` `supplementary` `materials` `references` `other` |  |
| `numbering-style` | kwarg | `arabic` `roman` `alpha` `none` |  |
| `+unlisted` | boolean | default `false` | Keep this sub-sub-section out of the generated table of contents, regardless of toc-depth (#218). Display-only: it still renders; it is only absent from the contents listing. See notes/specs/toc-and-numbering.md. Authored as +unlisted; renders to the HTML attribute unlisted. |
| `+unnumbered` | boolean | default `false` | Skip this sub-sub-section's number, regardless of number-depth (#218). Outside the numbered sequence — no number, no counter advance, subtree unnumbered; the next numbered sibling continues unbroken. See notes/specs/toc-and-numbering.md. Authored as +unnumbered. |

</section>

<section #sh-nav | `<nav>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *navigation*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<nav>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<nav>`

*No standalone example — see the registers above and the related constructs in this chapter.*

</section>

<section #sh-nav-group | `<nav-group>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *navigation-group*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<nav-group>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<nav-group>`

*No standalone example — see the registers above and the related constructs in this chapter.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | kwarg |  | The group's display label — its heading in the top bar (a dropdown) and the sidebar (an expandable node). Supplied as a kwarg, NOT a pipe: a `<nav-group>` is a long-form container (neither `\|` nor `/`), so the label cannot ride the pipe slot the way a page's title does. |

</section>

<section #sh-item | `<item>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *navigation-item*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<item>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<item>`

*No standalone example — see the registers above and the related constructs in this chapter.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `src` | kwarg |  | For an EXTERNAL page: the child `.emd` file that supplies the page body. The pipe gives the menu label and overrides the child's own title, exactly as `<section src \| Title>` does in an article. Omitted for an INLINE page, whose body is authored in the master after the `<item \| Title>` marker (peer-closed by the next entry, the `<section \| Title>` model). The website render (S2) loads the child; S1 records the `src` as a reference only. |

</section>

<section #sh-hr | `<hr>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *hr*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<hr>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<hr>`
- Markdown: `---`

<code #code:sh-hr-1>
<hr>
</code>

*Render pending — bare `<hr>` / `<hr type=…>` parsed as a long-form opener (no self-close) → tag-error (issue #275).*

<code #code:sh-hr-2>
<hr type=scene-break>
</code>

*Render pending — bare `<hr>` / `<hr type=…>` parsed as a long-form opener (no self-close) → tag-error (issue #275).*

<code #code:sh-hr-3>
<p | First paragraph.>

<hr type=ornamental>

<p | Second paragraph after a thematic break.>
</code>

*Render pending — bare `<hr>` / `<hr type=…>` parsed as a long-form opener (no self-close) → tag-error (issue #275).*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `scene-break` `section-break` `ornamental` `decorative` `other` | Optional classification of the thematic break's role. Affects rendering (scene breaks render as blank space; ornamental breaks render with decorative characters or images). |

</section>
